{"name":"Moon Shadow","hex":"#96a5b8","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"moon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Moon (“the Earth's only permanent natural satellite”)."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any natural satellite of a planet.","The light received from the Moon; moonlight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moon"},{"term":"shadow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dark image projected onto a surface where light (or other radiation) is blocked by the shade of an object.","Relative darkness, especially as caused by the interruption of light; gloom; obscurity."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Unofficial, informal, unauthorized, but acting as though it were.","Having power or influence, but not widely known or recognized."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shadow"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moonshadow (song)","blocks":[{"text":"\"Moonshadow\" is a song written and performed by English musician Cat Stevens, first released as a single in the UK in 1970 on the Island label and in the US in June 1971 on the A&M label. It also appears on Stevens' 1971 album Teaser and the Firecat."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c2/Moonshadow45.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moonshadow_(song)"},{"title":"Moon Shadow (album)","blocks":[{"text":"Moon Shadow is the second album by American singing trio Labelle. This release was their second and last album for Warner Bros. Records. The album is notable for their soulful rendition of The Who's \"Won't Get Fooled Again\", the socially conscious \"I Believe That I've Finally Made It Home\" and the nine-minute title track in which Patti introduces all the musicians as they do their live solos. This is the first album where member Nona Hendryx begins taking over most of the songwriting."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/56/LabelleMoonShadow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon_Shadow_(album)"},{"title":"Moon","blocks":[{"text":"The Moon is the only natural satellite of Earth. It orbits Earth in an elliptical path, at a distance that varies over the year, averaging around 378,000 kilometers (235,000 mi). It completes an orbit in relation to Earth and the Sun (synodically) every 29.5 days. The Moon and Earth are bound by gravitational attraction, which is stronger on the sides facing each other. The resulting tidal forces are the main driver of Earth's tides, and have pulled the Moon to always face Earth with the same near side. This tidal locking effectively synchronizes the Moon's rotation period to its orbital period."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/330px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moon"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c2/Moonshadow45.jpg/250px-Moonshadow45.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/c/c2/Moonshadow45.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/c/c2/Moonshadow45.jpg/250px-Moonshadow45.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMoonshadow45.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/56/LabelleMoonShadow.jpg/250px-LabelleMoonShadow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/5/56/LabelleMoonShadow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/5/56/LabelleMoonShadow.jpg/250px-LabelleMoonShadow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALabelleMoonShadow.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/500px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/1280px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/FullMoon2010.jpg/500px-FullMoon2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFullMoon2010.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Big_Splash_Theia.gif/500px-Big_Splash_Theia.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/Big_Splash_Theia.gif/500px-Big_Splash_Theia.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"This not-to-scale animation shows (in an Earth and Sun stationary rotating frame of reference) the formation of the Moon through the collision of the hypothetical Mars-sized planet named Theia with Earth. After orbiting for tens of millions of years at the L4 Lagrange point as an Earth-trojan, Theia's orbit comes in \"loops\" finally too close to Earth.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABig_Splash_Theia.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Archean.png/500px-Archean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Archean.png/1280px-Archean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Archean.png/500px-Archean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Artist's depiction of the Moon in Earth's sky as it might have appeared around 4 billion years ago, after the Late Heavy Bombardment. At that time the Moon orbited the Earth at a third of its current distance, making it appear 2.8 times larger in apparent size than it does today.[60]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AArchean.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Space.0076.fig.002.jpg/500px-Space.0076.fig.002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/Space.0076.fig.002.jpg/500px-Space.0076.fig.002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Moon was volcanically active for billions of years after its formation. Lava flooding the lunar low lying terrane over time formed today's basalt maria, with their ages marked in this map (yellow to red: youngest; blue to purple: oldest).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASpace.0076.fig.002.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg/500px-Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg/1280px-Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg/500px-Selected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Size comparison of the main moons of the Solar System with Earth to scale. Nineteen moons are large enough to be round, several having subsurface oceans and one, Titan, having a considerable atmosphere.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASelected_Moons_of_Solar_System.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Youtubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif/250px-Youtubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/eb/Youtubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Youtubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif/250px-Youtubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Astronaut John Young jumping on the Moon, illustrating that the gravitational pull of the Moon is approximately 1/6 of Earth's. The jumping height is limited by the combined weight of Young and his EVA space suit on the Moon of about 27 kg (60 lb) and by the suit's pressurization resisting the bending of the suit, as needed for jumping.[93][94]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AYoutubeastronautsonmoonot3.gif"}],"sounds":{"moon":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/fe/En-uk-moon.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/f/fe/En-uk-moon.ogg/En-uk-moon.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/93/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-moon.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/93/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-moon.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-moon.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/93/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-moon.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Back_ache-moon.